__d(function(e,a,t,i,n,r,o){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science",textbook:"Social and Political Life \u2014 Part I (Civics)",chapter_number:25,chapter_title:"Panchayati Raj",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is Panchayati Raj and which Constitutional Amendment established it?",back:"Panchayati Raj is India's three-tier system of local self-government in rural areas. It was given constitutional status by the 73rd Constitutional Amendment in 1992, making it mandatory for all states to set up elected local bodies at the village, block, and district levels.",memory_hook:"73 = 7+3 = Panchayati Raj's three tiers got their rights in 1992. The 73rd Amendment = democracy's third tier gets its home."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name the three tiers of Panchayati Raj from the village level to the district level.",back:"Tier 1 \u2014 Gram Panchayat (village level). Tier 2 \u2014 Panchayat Samiti / Taluk Panchayat (block level). Tier 3 \u2014 Zila Parishad / Zilla Panchayat (district level). In Karnataka: Grama Panchayat \u2192 Taluk Panchayat \u2192 Zilla Panchayat.",memory_hook:"VBD pyramid: Village \u2192 Block \u2192 District. Or: GP (small) \u2192 PS (medium) \u2192 ZP (large). From the field to the district office \u2014 three steps up."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a ward? Why is a village divided into wards?",back:"A ward is a small electoral division of a village. Dividing a village into wards ensures that representatives are elected from every part of the village \u2014 no area is left without a voice. Each ward elects one Panch (ward member) to the Gram Panchayat, ensuring proportional and geographically fair representation.",memory_hook:"Ward = the village's smallest democratic unit. Like dividing a school into houses for representation \u2014 each house gets a representative. Ward = the village's electoral postal code."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who is a Sarpanch and what is their role?",back:"The Sarpanch is the elected head (president) of the Gram Panchayat. They preside over Gram Panchayat meetings, represent the village in dealings with government officials and higher-tier bodies, and are responsible for implementing the Gram Panchayat's decisions. In Karnataka, the Sarpanch is called 'Adhyaksha' in some documents.",memory_hook:"Sarpa = head/chief in Sanskrit. Sarpanch = chief of the Panch (five-member council). The village's own elected chief minister \u2014 but for just one village."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Gram Sabha? How is it different from the Gram Panchayat?",back:"Gram Sabha: a meeting of ALL registered adult voters (18+) of the village. It is the most fundamental democratic body at the village level \u2014 every adult participates directly. Gram Panchayat: the ELECTED body of 5\u201315 Panchs (ward members) who run the village day-to-day. Key difference: Gram Panchayat = elected few. Gram Sabha = entire village. The Gram Sabha oversees and approves the Gram Panchayat's work.",memory_hook:"Panchayat = Parliament of the village (elected few). Sabha = the People (everyone). Sabha > Panchayat in ultimate authority \u2014 just as the people are above their elected representatives."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How often must the Gram Sabha meet? What does it discuss?",back:"The Gram Sabha must meet at least twice a year (minimum national requirement). Karnataka mandates four meetings per year. At each meeting, it: reviews the Gram Panchayat's work and accounts; approves the annual plan and budget; selects beneficiaries for welfare schemes (MGNREGA, PM Awas, PDS); conducts social audit of expenditures; and gives villagers a platform to raise local issues.",memory_hook:"Gram Sabha = village's Annual General Meeting \u2014 but twice (or four times) a year. The AGM where every shareholder (voter) gets a vote."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"List five functions of the Gram Panchayat.",back:"(1) Build and maintain village roads, drains, and culverts. (2) Manage water supply \u2014 village wells, hand pumps, taps. (3) Maintain sanitation \u2014 public toilets, garbage disposal. (4) Maintain primary school buildings and support Mid-Day Meal scheme. (5) Implement government welfare schemes \u2014 MGNREGA (job guarantee), PM Awas Yojana (housing), PDS (ration shops). Also: issue birth/death certificates, maintain streetlights, run anganwadi centres.",memory_hook:"GP functions = RIEWS+W: Roads, Infrastructure, Education, Water, Sanitation + Welfare schemes. What does a village need? The GP provides it."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the main sources of income for a Gram Panchayat?",back:"Three main income sources: (1) Own tax revenue \u2014 house tax (on buildings in the village), water tax, market/mela tax, vehicle tax. (2) State government grants \u2014 allocated by the State Finance Commission. (3) Central government grants \u2014 from the Central Finance Commission, MGNREGA funds, and other scheme allocations. The GP must manage this money transparently and present accounts at the Gram Sabha.",memory_hook:"GP income = TGG: Taxes (own), Government-state grants, Government-central grants. Three sources \u2014 own earning + state support + central support."},{id:"fc09",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does the 73rd Amendment reserve 33% of Panchayat seats for women?",back:"Women make up nearly half India's population but were historically excluded from political power. Reserving 33% seats ensures women are elected to Panchayats, not just as token gestures but as a guaranteed minimum. It: gives women political experience and voice in local governance; brings issues like water, safety, health, and education to the forefront (issues women prioritise); challenges social norms that prevent women from holding positions of authority; and builds a pipeline of women leaders in Indian democracy.",memory_hook:"33% = minimum \u2014 not ceiling. Women's reservation in Panchayats = democracy's affirmative promise that half the population will have a seat at the table."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why was Karnataka a pioneer in Panchayati Raj?",back:"Karnataka passed the Karnataka Zilla Parishads, Taluk Panchayat Samithis, Mandal Panchayats and Nyaya Panchayats Act in 1983 \u2014 nearly a decade before the 73rd Constitutional Amendment (1992) made it mandatory nationally. Karnataka was one of the first states to devolve real power, funds, and functions to local bodies and to implement reservations for women and SC/ST communities in local elections.",memory_hook:"Karnataka led India by 9 years: 1983 (Karnataka Panchayati Raj Act) vs 1992 (73rd Amendment). Karnataka = Panchayati Raj pioneer."},{id:"fc11",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a Social Audit and why is it important?",back:"Social Audit is a process where the Gram Panchayat's income and expenditure records are read aloud publicly at the Gram Sabha. Villagers can question any payment \u2014 e.g., a payment for road repairs that no one saw being done. It is important because: it prevents corruption; it makes the Gram Panchayat accountable to voters; it ensures public money is spent correctly; and it empowers ordinary villagers to act as watchdogs of their local government.",memory_hook:"Social Audit = public accounts reading. Government auditors check books in private; Gram Sabha checks them in public \u2014 in front of everyone who might have been cheated."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is the Gram Sabha considered more powerful than the Gram Panchayat?",back:"The Gram Sabha has ultimate democratic authority because it represents ALL voters \u2014 not just the elected few. It can: approve or reject the GP's plans; question any expenditure; demand explanation from the Sarpanch; and override the GP's decisions through collective voter power. The Gram Panchayat works between Sabha meetings but must answer to the Sabha. In democracy, the people (Sabha) are supreme over their representatives (Panchayat).",memory_hook:"Sabha = the Boss. Panchayat = the employee. The elected representative works for the people, not the other way around."},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is MGNREGA and how does the Gram Panchayat implement it?",back:"MGNREGA (Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act) is a central government law that guarantees 100 days of wage employment per year to every rural household that needs it. The Gram Panchayat is the key implementing agency: it registers job-seekers, provides employment (usually on public works like roads, ponds, tree-planting), maintains muster rolls (attendance), and pays wages. The Gram Sabha approves the work plans and the social audit checks if wages were actually paid.",memory_hook:"MGNREGA = 100 days guarantee. GP = the employment office of the village. Job-seekers go to their GP, not to the state capital."},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How is India's Panchayati Raj system an example of 'decentralisation' of power?",back:"Decentralisation means spreading power from a central authority to lower-level bodies. Panchayati Raj is decentralisation because: instead of the central or state government making all local decisions, power is given to local elected bodies (Gram Panchayats); funds are transferred to GPs to spend on local needs; and local administration (roads, water, schools) is handled by locally elected people who know local conditions best.",memory_hook:"Centralisation = all power in one place (capital). Decentralisation = power shared down to the village. Panchayati Raj = democracy's decentralisation achievement."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A Gram Panchayat collects house tax but uses the money to build a personal garage for the Sarpanch. What democratic mechanism can villagers use to challenge this?",back:"Villagers have two main mechanisms: (1) Gram Sabha \u2014 they can raise this issue at the next Gram Sabha meeting, where the Sarpanch must account for all expenditures. The Sabha can demand refund and removal from office. (2) Social Audit \u2014 during Social Audit at the Gram Sabha, every expenditure is read aloud; villagers can formally object to the garage expenditure and report it to higher authorities (Block Development Officer or Zilla Panchayat). Using tax money for personal benefit is corruption and can lead to the Sarpanch being removed.",memory_hook:"Corruption + Gram Sabha = accountability. The Gram Sabha is the village's anti-corruption committee. No Sarpanch can hide misuse when ALL voters meet and question accounts."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the role of the Panchayat Samiti (Block level)?",back:"The Panchayat Samiti is the middle tier covering a block (several villages). Its functions: supervises and supports the Gram Panchayats in its area; implements block-level development schemes (secondary schools, block-level roads, health centres); coordinates between Gram Panchayats and the Zila Parishad; and channels funds and technical support to Gram Panchayats. In Karnataka, this tier is called the Taluk Panchayat.",memory_hook:"Panchayat Samiti = bridge between village and district. GP handles the village. ZP handles the district. PS handles everything in between."},{id:"fc17",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Grama One initiative in Karnataka and how does it connect to Panchayati Raj?",back:"Grama One (launched by Karnataka government) is an initiative that provides over 90 government services directly at the Gram Panchayat level \u2014 services like caste certificates, income certificates, ration card updates, MGNREGA registrations, and property tax payments. It strengthens Panchayati Raj by making the GP a genuine one-stop service centre for villagers, reducing the need to travel to taluk or district offices for routine services.",memory_hook:"Grama One = 90+ services at your doorstep. Instead of 90 trips to different offices, one trip to the Gram Panchayat. Karnataka's digital Panchayati Raj in action."},{id:"fc18",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How are SC and ST communities protected in Panchayati Raj elections?",back:"The 73rd Amendment and subsequent state laws reserve a proportion of seats in Panchayats for Scheduled Caste (SC) and Scheduled Tribe (ST) communities proportional to their population in the area. For example, if SC communities form 20% of a village's population, 20% of ward seats are reserved for SC candidates. This ensures historically marginalised communities have guaranteed representation in local governance and cannot be shut out by dominant caste groups.",memory_hook:"Population proportion = reservation proportion. SC/ST reservation in Panchayats = their population % reflected in seats. Democracy guarantees representation to those historically denied it."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A family in a village wants to apply for a house under PM Awas Yojana (government housing scheme). What is the Gram Panchayat's role in this process?",back:"The Gram Panchayat plays a central role: (1) At a Gram Sabha meeting, the GP presents the list of eligible families (based on poverty criteria) and seeks approval from all voters \u2014 ensuring transparency. (2) The approved names are submitted by the Gram Panchayat to the Block Office (Panchayat Samiti level). (3) The GP then supervises construction, verifies completion, and maintains records. The Gram Sabha's approval prevents powerful families from grabbing benefits meant for the poor.",memory_hook:"Welfare scheme flow: GP identifies \u2192 Gram Sabha approves \u2192 Block processes \u2192 house built \u2192 GP verifies. Gram Panchayat is the first and last mile of welfare delivery."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is it said that the Gram Sabha is 'direct democracy' while the Gram Panchayat is 'representative democracy'?",back:"Direct democracy: every citizen participates in decisions personally. The Gram Sabha is direct democracy because ALL adult voters attend, speak, and vote on village issues \u2014 no intermediary. Representative democracy: citizens elect representatives who decide on their behalf. The Gram Panchayat is representative democracy \u2014 voters elect Panchs and Sarpanch who manage village affairs between Sabha meetings. India's national Parliament is also representative democracy. The Gram Sabha is one of the rare examples of direct democracy functioning at scale in India.",memory_hook:"Direct democracy = you decide (Sabha). Representative democracy = your chosen person decides (Panchayat/Parliament). The Gram Sabha is India's 'town hall meeting' \u2014 direct democracy in action."}]}},2934,[]);